
# Structure fixtures whose SHA-256 is recorded as gold-case provenance, and
# checked against on every verifier run. Git's default text handling would
# rewrite LF to CRLF when checking out on Windows (GitHub's runners set
# core.autocrlf=true), changing the bytes and so the digest -- which is
# exactly what broke //tests/data:generate_real_pymol and
# //tests/data:gold_case_verifier the first time they ran on windows-2025,
# with "structure checksum mismatch" rather than anything to do with the
# targets themselves. Marking them -text pins the working-tree bytes to
# what is committed on every platform, so a recorded digest means the same
# thing everywhere. This applies to the H-02 discovery fixture too, whose
# digest is recorded in docs/codev/wave/evidence/H-02.md.
*.pdb -text
#
# A clone made before this rule landed already holds CRLF copies in its
# working tree, and pulling will not re-check-out files whose blob did not
# change. Note that `git add --renormalize` is the wrong remedy here: under
# `-text` it cleans without converting, so it would commit the CRLF bytes
# and break the recorded digests for everyone. Force a re-checkout instead
# (delete the affected files and `git checkout -- .`). Note also that
# `-text` removes the conversion in both directions, so `core.autocrlf` no
# longer scrubs CRLF back to LF on the way in -- which is the point, but it
# means the working tree is now the only place this can go wrong.

# The committed gold set and the frozen split's record (master plan item
# 15) are hashed the same way: docs/dataset/manifest.json records the
# SHA-256 of the gold items and samples, and pins the audit sheet's, and
# tests/data/test_manifest.py checks those digests on every run. CRLF
# checkouts broke that on windows-2025 exactly as they broke the .pdb
# fixtures above, so the same rule applies.
src/pmc_data/gold/*.jsonl -text
docs/dataset/** -text
#
# The manifest also records the SHA-256 of the generation configs it was
# built from, and tests/data/test_manifest.py checks those against the
# working tree, so they need the same rule.
configs/generation/*.json -text
